    {
      "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "1052B Directions to increase limits on the value of claims",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2022c00235#sch-1-sec-1052b"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "c2022c00235.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "c2022c00235.pdf, p.16",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If, under the AFCA scheme, there are one or more limits on:\n\nExternal dispute resolution  Schedule 1\nAmendments applying from the day after Royal Assent  Part 1\n\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Putting Consumers First—Establishment of the\nAustralian Financial Complaints Authority) Act 2018\nCompilation date: 10/08/2022\nRegistered: 25/08/2022\n\n(a) the value of claims that may be made under the scheme; or\n\n(b) the value of remedies that AFCA may determine under the\nscheme;\nASIC may give AFCA a written direction requiring the limit, or\nsome or all of the limits, to be increased.\nNote:\nOne of the operational requirements for the scheme is that there are no\nsuch limits for superannuation complaints (see paragraph 1051(4)(f)).\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) ASIC must not give a direction under this section unless\nASIC has given AFCA written notice of at least 1 month of\nASIC’s intention to issue the direction; and\n\n(b) a direction under this section must not apply in relation to\ncomplaints AFCA received before ASIC gives the direction.\n\n(3) AFCA must comply with a direction made under this section.\nNote:\nFailure to comply with this subsection is an offence (see\nsubsection 1311(1)).\n\n(4) If AFCA fails to comply with the direction, ASIC may apply to the\nCourt for, and the Court may make, an order that AFCA comply\nwith the direction.\n\n(5) A direction made under this section is not a legislative instrument.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If, under the AFCA scheme, there are one or more limits on:\n\nExternal dispute resolution  Schedule 1\nAmendments applying from the day after Royal Assent  Part 1\n\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Putting Consumers First—Establishment of the\nAustralian Financial Complaints Authority) Act 2018\nCompilation date: 10/08/2022\nRegistered: 25/08/2022\n\n(a) the value of claims that may be made under the scheme; or\n\n(b) the value of remedies that AFCA may determine under the\nscheme;\nASIC may give AFCA a written direction requiring the limit, or\nsome or all of the limits, to be increased.\nNote:\nOne of the operational requirements for the scheme is that there are no\nsuch limits for superannuation complaints (see paragraph 1051(4)(f)).\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) ASIC must not give a direction under this section unless\nASIC has given AFCA written notice of at least 1 month of\nASIC’s intention to issue the direction; and\n\n(b) a direction under this section must not apply in relation to\ncomplaints AFCA received before ASIC gives the direction.\n\n(3) AFCA must comply with a direction made under this section.\nNote:\nFailure to comply with this subsection is an offence (see\nsubsection 1311(1)).\n\n(4) If AFCA fails to comply with the direction, ASIC may apply to the\nCourt for, and the Court may make, an order that AFCA comply\nwith the direction.\n\n(5) A direction made under this section is not a legislative instrument.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2022c00235-sch-1-sec-1052b-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b841b89244d912f79674cf9cf309c69c921614ecd458283ea417d48ed6c5d38b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2022c00235#sch-1-sec-1052b",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "1052B Directions to increase limits on the value of claims",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 16,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If, under the AFCA scheme, there are one or more limits on:\n\nExternal dispute resolution  Schedule 1\nAmendments applying from the day after Royal Assent  Part 1\n\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Putting Consumers First—Establishment of the\nAustralian Financial Complaints Authority) Act 2018\nCompilation date: 10/08/2022\nRegistered: 25/08/2022\n\n(a) the value of claims that may be made under the scheme; or\n\n(b) the value of remedies that AFCA may determine under the\nscheme;\nASIC may give AFCA a written direction requiring the limit, or\nsome or all of the limits, to be increased.\nNote:\nOne of the operational requirements for the scheme is that there are no\nsuch limits for superannuation complaints (see paragraph 1051(4)(f)).\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) ASIC must not give a direction under this section unless\nASIC has given AFCA written notice of at least 1 month of\nASIC’s intention to issue the direction; and\n\n(b) a direction under this section must not apply in relation to\ncomplaints AFCA received before ASIC gives the direction.\n\n(3) AFCA must comply with a direction made under this section.\nNote:\nFailure to comply with this subsection is an offence (see\nsubsection 1311(1)).\n\n(4) If AFCA fails to comply with the direction, ASIC may apply to the\nCourt for, and the Court may make, an order that AFCA comply\nwith the direction.\n\n(5) A direction made under this section is not a legislative instrument."
    },
